  <h2>Purpose</h2>
  <p>
    St. Paul's Mission Hospital is committed to safeguarding every child and
    vulnerable adult who comes into contact with our services, staff,
    volunteers, contractors, and community programmes. This policy defines how
    we prevent harm, respond to concerns, and protect those who report.
  </p>
  <p>
    The policy applies to all staff, visiting clinicians, students, volunteers,
    contractors, and any person acting on behalf of SPMH. It also aligns with
    the safeguarding requirements of the Catholic Diocese of Homa Bay, the
    Catholic Health Commission of Kenya, and our development partners.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>This policy covers the protection of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Children</strong> — any person under 18 years of age.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vulnerable adults</strong> — any person aged 18 or over who, by reason
      of mental or other disability, age, or illness, is unable to protect themselves
      from significant harm or exploitation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Patients and their families</strong> — in any interaction with the hospital,
      including outreach, community programmes, and home-based care.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Definitions of harm</h2>
  <p>Safeguarding concerns include, but are not limited to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Physical abuse</strong> — non-accidental harm, including hitting, shaking,
      burning, or restraint.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sexual abuse</strong> — any sexual act or exploitation involving a child
      or vulnerable adult, whether or not force is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Emotional or psychological abuse</strong> — threats, humiliation, isolation,
      or persistent criticism causing distress.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Neglect</strong> — failure to provide basic needs such as food, shelter,
      medical care, or supervision.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Financial exploitation</strong> — misuse of a person's money, property,
      or entitlements.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gender-based violence</strong> — including intimate partner violence,
      sexual harassment, and harmful traditional practices.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Discrimination</strong> — including on the basis of gender, disability,
      ethnicity, religion, or HIV status.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Our commitments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We will screen all staff, volunteers, and contractors before engagement,
      including criminal record checks where permissible by law.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will train all staff on safeguarding at induction and annually
      thereafter.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will create a culture where concerns can be raised openly, without fear
      of retaliation.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will respond to every concern promptly, fairly, and in the best
      interest of the person at risk.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will cooperate fully with statutory authorities, including the
      Department of Children's Services and the police.
    </li>
    <li>
      We will maintain confidential safeguarding records, kept securely and
      separately from clinical records.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Code of conduct — for all staff and visitors</h2>

  <h3>You must:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Treat every child and vulnerable adult with respect and dignity.</li>
    <li>Maintain appropriate physical and emotional boundaries.</li>
    <li>
      Conduct any interaction with a child in the presence of another adult
      where possible.
    </li>
    <li>Report any concern, suspicion, or disclosure immediately.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>You must not:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Engage in any form of physical, sexual, emotional, or financial abuse.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use language or behaviour that is threatening, humiliating, or sexually
      suggestive.
    </li>
    <li>
      Share images or information about a patient on social media without
      documented consent.
    </li>
    <li>Transport a child or vulnerable adult alone in a private vehicle.</li>
    <li>
      Give gifts, money, or personal contact details to a child or vulnerable
      adult without authorization.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reporting procedure</h2>

  <h3>Step 1 — Report immediately</h3>
  <p>
    Any staff member, volunteer, patient, or visitor who has a safeguarding
    concern must report it <strong>immediately</strong> — never later than 24 hours
    after becoming aware.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2 — Report to the Safeguarding Focal Person</h3>
  <p>Reports may be made to any of the following:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The on-duty nurse-in-charge</li>
    <li>
      The Safeguarding Focal Person
    </li>
    <li>The Hospital Administrator's office</li>
    <li>The Catholic Diocese of Homa Bay safeguarding office</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Step 3 — Immediate protection</h3>
  <p>
    If the person at risk is in immediate danger, we will take necessary steps
    to secure their safety, including contacting the police, the Department of
    Children's Services, or medical emergency services.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4 — Investigation and follow-up</h3>
  <p>
    All reports are documented, investigated promptly, and followed up with the
    person who reported and (where appropriate) the person affected. Allegations
    involving staff are handled according to HR disciplinary procedures, and may
    be referred to statutory authorities.
  </p>

  <div class='callout'>
    <strong>Confidential reporting:</strong> All reports are treated as confidential.
    Anonymous reports are accepted. The identity of the reporter will not be shared
    with the person(s) being reported without the reporter's consent, unless legally
    required.
  </div>

  <h2>Protection for reporters</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who raises a safeguarding concern in good faith will be protected
    from retaliation, victimization, or any form of disadvantage. False or
    malicious reports made deliberately may be dealt with as a disciplinary
    matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Review</h2>
  <p>
    This policy is reviewed annually by the Safeguarding Committee and approved
    by the Board of Management. It is aligned with the <em
      >Kenya Children Act 2022</em
    >, the
    <em>Kenya Persons with Disabilities Act 2003</em>, and the <em
      >Catholic Church's Safeguarding Framework</em
    >.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
